            <ArticleTitle>Pain Management for Children during Bone Marrow and Stem Cell Transplantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>156-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmn.2014.05.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1524-9042(14)00089-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pain management for children during bone marrow and stem cell transplantation is a significant clinical challenge for the health care team. Pain management strategies vary by institution. This paper reports on the use of a pediatric pain management service and patient- and caregiver-controlled analgesia for children undergoing transplant. This 2-year retrospective chart review examined the pain management practices and outcomes of children undergoing bone marrow and stem cell transplants in a large urban teaching hospital during 2008 and 2009. We concluded that patient- and caregiver-controlled analgesia is a well-tolerated modality for pain control during hospitalization for transplantation at this institution.</AbstractText>
